## MOF thin films: existing and future applicationsw
## 1. Introduction
The functionalization of surfaces and interfaces is a topic of pronounced technological importance. Adjusting, augmenting, improving or changing the properties of a surface with regard to its optical, electrical, tribological, wetting or biological properties are key issues with regard to rendering a desired functionality to an object. For more advanced applications employing the hierarchical structures fundamental to Interfacial Systems Chemistry,1 it becomes more and more important to carry out this surface functionalization in a very flexible way with the ultimate goal to create surfaces with properties which can be adjusted and modified by remote control.
With regard to achieving a maximum degree of flexibility the coating of a surface with a thin layer of a porous material offers an attractive potential, since such a molecular shelf grafted to a surface makes it possible to render the desired functionality to the substrate by loading the pores of the molecular scaffold with functional molecules—e.g. dyes, molecular magnets or metallic nanoparticles. If the flexibility of the molecular scaffold is sufficient, this loading can be carried out in either an irreversible or reversible fashion.
Porous materials are defined as solids containing empty voids which can host other molecules. The fundamental feature of these materials is their porosity, the ratio between total occupied and empty space, the (average) size of the pores and the surface area. Typical values for the surface area of porous materials applied in technological processes range between 2000 and $8000~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .2 Most important applications of such materials are the storage of small molecules (e.g. loading a bathing sponge with water) and filtering (e.g. using as a sieve e.g. in connection with cleaning of water). The framework defining a porous material can consist of either organic or inorganic materials. With regard to developing new methods to produce novel, highly flexible functional materials the loading of porous materials with functional molecules or objects is attracting an increasing amount of interest, e.g. loading a porous material with metal particles which can then be used in heterogeneous catalysis.3
The most common organic porous substance is activated carbon, a technologically very important material with surface areas above $2000\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and very high adsorption capacities for a variety of gases and molecules.4 One of the most important applications of activated carbon are gas masks, where the ability to adsorb active molecules on the high surface exposed by the walls of the pores is used to clean air before breathing. A characteristic feature of activated carbon is that the pores are not ordered and do not have fixed sizes, the diameter of the pores varies within a range of values and also their shapes very strongly.4
Another important type of very common porous materials are zeolites, inorganic porous frameworks which exhibit a high degree of molecular order. Nano- or mesoporous zeolites are contained in many types of soaps used in washing machines to clean textiles. This class of materials mainly consist of Al, Si and chalcogens and posses a certain degree of diversity. About 70 different types of zeolites are known today.6 As is the case of activated carbon, zeolites have numerous applications in gas storage, adsorption, gas/vapor separation and catalysis,7–10 although the most important applications result from their ion-exchange properties.11
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are a new class of porous materials of hybrid organic/inorganic nature that allow combining the properties of both organic and inorganic porous materials.12–14 MOFs are stable up to temperatures typically above $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , exhibit a high degree of crystallinity and are characterized by very high surface areas. The maximum degree of porosity as well as of pore sizes clearly exceeds that of zeolites.
MOFs are essentially coordination polymers formed by connecting metal ions with polytopic organic linkers resulting in often fascinating structural topologies. These materials have attracted a great deal of attention in the past decade. Originally largely driven by the prospect of gas-storage (in particular $\mathrm{H}_{2}^{\ 15}$ ) presently the development in this area is driven by the prospect of using this interesting class of materials for other purposes. Applications for gas/vapor separation, size-, shape-, and enantioselective catalysis, fabricating luminescent and fluorescent materials, and drug storage and delivery have either already been demonstrated or proposed.16–19
The topic of this review is the preparation and application of continuous, thin porous MOF films, either supported on solid substrates or as free-standing membranes. The availability of such highly porous thin films provides a number of new perspectives for application of these materials. The presence of a solid support to which the MOF-films are rigidly anchored creates possibilities not available for the typical MOF powders obtained by the normal solvothermal bulk synthesis scheme with particle sizes of a few $\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . Examples for the applications of such supported MOF films are optical coatings, catalysis, gas separation or sensor application. Because the demand for fabricating such porous coatings is rather obvious, in the past years several synthesis schemes have been developed for the preparation of MOF thin porous films. A previous, rather short review has described the state of the art in this field up to the year 2008.20 Since then the field of porous coordination polymers in general and the corresponding thin films has seen rapid development as well as a substantial increase in activities and in the number of papers published on this topic. Many new groups have focused their efforts on the preparations of supported MOF thin films and have developed a number of new methods to prepare such films. A number of works have either demonstrated or proposed new applications of MOF thin films. The present review focuses on these more recent efforts and considers developments up to the year 2010. We will concentrate on applications of MOF thin films and point out implications for the corresponding film deposition methods. The very interesting aspects of MOF surface chemistry, considering the (outer) surfaces of bulk powder particles as well as those of supported thin films are the topic of another recent review article.21
## 1.1 Porous thin films fabricated from zeolites
Before we focus on MOF thin films, the main topic of this article, we will first provide a short description of the situation for zeolite thin films. This type of porous materials has been known for a much longer time than MOFs and, accordingly, a number of approaches to create zeolite thin films have been reported. A number of reviews are available in this area, e.g. Bein,22 Feng and $\mathrm{Bein}^{23}$ as well as Valtchev and Mintova.24
The most direct approach for the deposition of zeolite thin films on a solid substrate is the coating of a substrate by a colloidal suspension of zeolite powder particles, e.g. using spin-coating.25 A number of variants of this method have been reported, see the review by Yoon.26 One obvious disadvantage of this deposition method is that the zeolite films are not rigidly bound to the substrate. In the past, several methods have been proposed to achieve a covalent anchoring of zeolite crystals to the substrate, including the post-synthetic attachment of powder particles to an appropriately functionalized substrate, a method which has first been demonstrated for zeolite-A crystals like ZSM-5.19,26–28 Certain disadvantages of this somewhat indirect method (see discussion below) can be avoided by finding conditions under which a direct growth of zeolite-type materials on appropriately functionalized substrates becomes possible. Such a process somewhat similar to biomineralisation29–31 has the potential of generating more homogenous films and to control the orientation of the grown crystals with a regard for the substrate.23,32,33
For applications in optoelectronics it is of interest to deposit oriented monolayers of e.g. zeolite L crystals (crystal sizes in the $10{-}100\ \mathrm{nm}$ regime) on different types of substrates (Si, $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ , ITO, Au) and a number of approaches to realize this goal have been published.26,32,34–36 A prerequisite for these deposition methods is the fact that by controlling the synthesis of L-type zeolites nm- to $\upmu\mathrm{m}$ -sized platelets with different aspect ratios can be prepared. Recently, monolayers of oriented zeolite-crystals with thicknesses of about $50\ \mathrm{nm}$ were prepared by soft lithography employing microcontact transfer printing,37 a method which is generally applicable for the transfer of planar objects onto a substrate.38,39 This method also allows for the patterned deposition of zeolite films.
## 1.2 Thin porous films made from metal–organic frameworks (MOFs)
We now turn our attention to MOFs. Similar to the case of zeolites discussed above, the indirect route of anchoring of MOF powder particles directly from the solution can be used.
The first successful demonstration reported in the literature was carried out for MOF-5; crystallites from this material could be anchored rigidly and selectively on modified gold substrates.3 Because of the somewhat milder synthesis condition for MOFs compared to zeolites, a direct immersion of correspondingly functionalized organic surfaces into slightly aged MOF synthesis solutions (‘‘mother liquor’’) can be employed. The potential of this approach was first demonstrated for the case of a microporous manganese(II) formate framework by Arnold et al. on porous substrates.40
Because of the pronounced interest in the fabrication of well defined and highly porous coatings recently a number of new or modified methods for the deposition of MOF thin films have been developed and were shown to yield stable thin films on a number of different substrates. So far four different concepts have been employed for the growth of MOF thin films on solid substrates: (A) the direct growth/deposition from solvothermal mother solutions, (B) the assembly of preformed, size selected nanocrystals, (C) the stepwise layer-by-layer growth onto the substrate, (D) the electrochemical deposition of thin MOF-films on metal substrates and (E) the deposition of MOF thin films using a gel-layer approach. These approaches will be discussed in some more detail below.
A field related to the deposition of thin films on a substrate is the growth of one MOF on another type of MOF (MOFon-MOF). The so far fairly limited research on the deposition of MOFs on MOF nanocrystals and/or MOF colloids has recently been reviewed by Spokoyny et al.41
After presenting a short review of the existing methods to prepare MOF thin films the present review will address some of the potential and applications of thin films in different fields like luminescence, QCM-based sensors, organic electronics and catalysis. We will also point out implications of particular applications for the preparation method. A separate chapter will be devoted to the delamination of MOF thin films and discuss the potential to use them as free-standing membranes. The review will also demonstrate the potential of using MOF thin films as model systems for detailed studies on MOF-related phenomena, e.g. adsorption and diffusion of small molecules into MOFs as well as the formation mechanism of MOFs.
## 2. Methods for the fabrication of MOF thin films
As mentioned in the introduction, the rather flexible natures of MOF thin films have triggered the development of a number of different approaches for MOF deposition on solid substrates. In the following we will describe these strategies using selected examples. The differences between these different methods concern first the type of substrate used and second the deposition procedure itself. Depositions have been reported on native (e.g. oxydic) substrates as well as on particularly functionalized surfaces (functionalization with either thiolateor silane-based SAMs). The deposition itself can be carried out by either anchoring powder particles already existing in solution, by grafting small (nm-sized) seed crystals and then exposing to a solution containing the reactants for MOF synthesis, by using electrochemical methods, or, finally, by employing a substantially different process, liquid phase epitaxy (LPE). In the following these different procedures will be discussed separately.
Before we come to the description of the deposition procedure we would like to point out that the requirements on the substrates onto which the MOF thin films are going to be deposited depend strongly on the different preparation methods. The most direct approach, a simple coating with MOF-particles by dipping suitable substrates into a suspension of MOF powder particles and then letting the solvent evaporate, puts no special requirements on the supporting surface. This straightforward method, however, carries the disadvantage that the deposition is not selective, cannot be used to obtain monolithic membranes and also does not lead to a rigid anchoring of the crystallites on the substrate.42
For some of the other methods, in particular for the LPE-method, particularly functionalized substrates have to be used to direct the nucleation, orientation and structure of the deposited MOFs.43,44 In fact, the control of crystallization of inorganic solid state materials by the influence of organic macromolecules is the underlying principle of biomineralisation;45 the examples presented below demonstrate that a similar approach also works for anchoring MOFs to organic surfaces.
## 2.1 Growth/deposition from solvothermal mother solutions
This straightforward approach employed in the first successful preparation of well defined MOF thin films,46 is based on the conventional MOF synthesis scheme using solvothermal methods. Briefly, in the case of MOF-5, the conventional synthesis scheme is as follows: after mixing the reactants $(Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}–4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , terephthalic acid) the solution is kept at elevated temperatures for an extended amount of time $\mathrm{105^{\circ}C}$ , 1 atm). After a period of 3 days the reactions product was a powder consisting of particles precipitates at the bottom of the container. When aiming at a rigid deposition of the MOF particles on a substrate the most naive approach would be to simply immerse a substrate into the solution during MOF formation.
In order to obtain such a grafting of MOFs onto a solid substrate, first a suitable surface functionalization has to be chosen. Most importantly, the termination functional groups of this surface should allow for a direct binding of the MOF material. Realizing that many MOFs and also MOF-5 are based on carboxylate–metal bonds, an obvious choice are carboxylic acid terminated organic surfaces. The most simple way to prepare organic surfaces with such a termination is to employ self-assembled monolayers, SAMs.43 SAMs are ultrathin organic thin films with a very well defined molecular arrangement and a high structural quality which have found a widespread application in the past two decades and were also used as model systems for organic surfaces.43,47 A main advantage of SAMs is the fact that they can be easily fabricated by immersing Au-substrates into solutions of o-functionalized organothiols. After a few hours densely packed, highly ordered and oriented monolayers are formed spontaneously on the Au-surface. Although the precise packing density and type of defects depends on the particular organothiols used and also to some extent on preparation conditions,48 in many cases the structural quality of the SAMs is only limited by that of the substrate, for single-crystalline Au-substrates domain sizes of several $\upmu\mathrm{m}$ can be reached.49 By attaching an appropriate functionality $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ at the $\boldsymbol{\omega}$ -position of organothiols, organic surfaces terminated by $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ can be prepared in a straightforward fashion. Note, however, that interactions between the functional groups $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{X}$ can lead to severe complications, see ref. 47.
Following the considerations presented above the naive approach to grow MOFs on a substrate would therefore consist of immersing a COOH-terminated SAM into the normal MOF reactant solution and then use the normal solvothermal synthesis conditions. In principle, the COOH-groups exposed at the SAM-surface could serve as reactants and replace the COOH-groups of the organic ligand (benzenetricarboxylic acid, or $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{btc}$ , in the case of HKUST1). There is, however, a major problem with this approach: the normal synthesis temperature used for MOF-5 synthesis, $105^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , exceeds the temperature range in which thiolatebased SAMs are stable (in vacuum desorption is observed at temperatures around $90\mathrm{~\ensuremath~{~}~}^{\circ}\mathbf{C}^{48})$ by more than $20\mathrm{~\ensuremath~{~\circ~}~}_{\bf C}$ . Accordingly, immersion of a thiolate-based SAM into the MOF-5 reaction solution would lead to a thermal decomposition of the SAM and or at least no selective deposition of MOF material would occur.
In order to account for this problem, in 2005 Hermes et al. have employed a more sophisticated scheme.46 After mixing the reactants for the solvothermal synthesis of MOF-5, the solution was kept at the high temperature demanded by the normal reaction protocol for a short time only ( $^{72\mathrm{{h}}}$ instead of $\mathord{\sim}7$ days for the conventional synthesis). The liquid was then filtered and allowed to cool down to room temperature. In the next step, the COOH-terminated SAM was immersed into this nanometre sized MOF-5 nuclei containing ‘‘mother solution’’. After an immersion time of $24\mathrm{~h~}$ the formation of rigidly anchored crystallites on the organic surface exposed by the SAM could be achieved, as demonstrated by the optical micrographs shown in Fig. 1. The lateral patterning of the deposited crystallites was achieved by employing the method of micro-contact printing where a stamp consisting of the polymer PMMA is gently inked with the COOH-terminated thiol and then gently pressed against the Au-substrate.50 After this stamping process the sample was immersed in a second, $\mathrm{CF}_{3}$ -terminated organothiol yielding a SAM consisting of COOH-terminated squares and $\mathrm{CF}_{3}$ -terminated stripes separating them.
The images presented in Fig. 1 show clearly that an immersion of such patterned, mixed –COOH and $-\mathrm{CF}_{3}.$ - terminated, SAM organic substrate in the synthesis solution (see above) results in the selective deposition of well-defined MOF-5 cubes with sizes of $1{-}10~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ grafted onto the squares on the substrate.46 The fact that obviously the deposition of MOF particles only occurs on the squares where the SAM exhibits a COOH-terminated surface and not on the stripes which exhibit a $\mathrm{CF}_{3}$ -terminated surface clearly demonstrates that the concept of providing anchoring sites at the SAM surface has worked beautifully. Obviously, in the formation process, the MOF material accepts the exposed carboxylic acid groups as binding sites comparable to those exposed by the organic ligand in the MOF-5 synthesis, but cannot bind to the $\mathrm{CF}_{3}$ -terminated parts of the SAM surface. For applications of MOF thin films, this possibility to obtain a selective deposition of course is very important (see below).
In order to demonstrate the porous properties of the adsorbed thin film the deposited MOF material was exposed to the vapor of an organometallic palladium precursor, Cp-allyl-Pd, which has been shown in previous work to diffuse into MOF-5 powder particles.46
The color change noted after exposing the deposited thin films to Cp-allyl-Pd provides a strong indication that the thin films are indeed highly porous. As previously demonstrated for bulk powder samples Cp-Allyl-Pd is included in the pores of MOF-5 and yields a reddish color of the infiltrated MOF lattice. The same color is observed for the deposited MOF-5 film. Of course, for an unambiguous proof that the deposited are structurally identical to MOF-5 more quantitative evidence is needed. The $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction (XRD) data shown in Fig. 2 clearly demonstrates that the deposited material is indeed MOF-5. In the $\theta-2\theta$ scans the diffraction peaks are located at the same positions as for the bulk samples and also the relative intensities of the peaks are the same as in XRD data recorded for powder samples using the conventional, solvothermal synthesis scheme. The fact that in the $\theta-2\theta$ XRD scan (later referred to as ‘‘out of plane’’ diffraction scans) peaks for all orientations are seen to reveal that the deposited MOF films are polycrystalline and consist of randomly oriented crystallites. The thickness of the MOF coating corresponds to the size of single crystallites which amount to a few $\upmu\mathrm{m}$ .
## 2.2 The direct oriented growth/deposition from solvothermal mother solutions
A beautiful experiment demonstrating that the potential of SAMs goes well beyond just providing nucleation centers for the (selective) growth of polycrystalline MOF thin films has been reported by Bein and coworkers51 for the case of a different MOF consisting of benzenetricarboxylate (btc) and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions, HKUST-1. In their study they used three different terminations, $-\mathrm{COOH}$ , $-\mathrm{OH}$ and $-\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ , for the substrate SAMs. Somewhat similar to the method proposed by Hermes et al. (see previous paragraph) first an aged (8 d at $75^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ ) and filtered ‘‘mother’’ solution was prepared which was allowed to cool down to room temperature before immersion of the SAMs. In contrast to the results by Hermes et al. for MOF-5 discussed in the previous paragraph in this case the $\theta-2\theta$ out-of-plane scans showed only a subset of the diffraction peaks present in the bulk powder XRD data. For example, in the case of COOH-terminated SAM the $\theta-2\theta$ scans (see Fig. 3) reveal diffraction peaks at the same positions as in the XRD data recorded for the bulk samples but only the peaks belonging to the [100] crystallographic direction are present (i.e. [100], [200], [300],. . .). Peaks belonging to other crystallographic orientations, e.g. the [111] peak, were absent in the $\theta-2\theta$ scans recorded for MOF-layers deposited on the COOH-terminated SAMs. Interestingly, the situation was quite different for the case of the OH-terminated SAM. For this differently terminated organic substrate only peaks belonging to the [111] crystallographic direction were seen (e.g. [111], [222], [333]), whereas all other peaks, including the [100] direction peak, were missing. These results demonstrate that the crystallites deposited on the SAMs are oriented with their [100] and [111] direction perpendicular to the substrate for the COOH- and OH-terminated SAMs, respectively.
Similar findings were reported independently at about the same time by Zacher et al.,52 who also studied the deposition of HKUST-1 on a different type of SAM, namely COOH- and $\mathrm{CF}_{3}$ -terminated (patterned) silane based SAMs deposited on $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}/\mathrm{Si}$ -substrates, as well as on c-plane sapphire. In contrast to the method used by Bein et al., in this case the higher thermal stability of the silane-based SAMs allowed to immerse the organic substrates directly into containers where the reactants were kept under conventional bulk solvothermal growth conditions at $110{-}120\ ^{\circ}\mathbf{C}.^{52}$ Also in the study of Zacher et al., a highly selective and oriented growth of HKUST-1 on the COOH-terminated sites of the SAMs was observed. Interestingly, however, they reported the opposite orientations: [100] perpendicular to the surface for the COOH-terminated organic surface and the [111] perpendicular to the surface for the OH-terminated organic surface.
The results reported in this paper are important since they demonstrate that a selective deposition of MOFs is not limited to organic surfaces exposed by thiolate-based SAMs on Au-substrates. The possibility to use silane-based SAMs substantially extends the number of materials which can be used as substrates. A particular advantage is that silanes can also be deposited on insulating, transparent surfaces, e.g. glass, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , other oxides, whereas thiolate-based SAMs are deposited on Au-substrates which are not transparent to visible light.
## 2.3 Microwave-induced thermal deposition
Microwave-induced thermal deposition (MITD) is another novel method to prepare MOF thin films on porous substrates in a facile manner. The selective patterned deposition of MOF-5 crystals as well as a multi-layer film (an MOF-5/ zeolite silicalite-1 film) on a substrate were demonstrated by Yoo et al. with this method.53,54 The MOF-5 precursor solution was prepared according to a previously reported procedure.54 Substrates (nanoporous anodized alumina discs) coated with various conductive thin films were then placed vertically in vials containing the precursor solution. Subsequently, MOF-5 crystals were grown under microwave irradiation in a domestic microwave oven with $500~\mathrm{W}$ power for 5–30 s (Fig. 4). The fundamental basis of this method is to create the high temperatures needed for the MOF formation only in the vicinity of the surface. As a result the reaction does not proceed within the solution but only in a rather thin layer at the interface to the substrate heated by the microwave radiation.
Thin layers of conductive materials such as amorphous carbon, graphite, and other metals such as Au were found to drastically enhance the kinetics of heterogeneous nucleation and growth of MOF-5 crystals.
## 2.4 Colloidal deposition method
A rather straightforward approach for the deposition of MOF crystallites has been demonstrated by Ferey et al. for the flexible, porous iron carboxylate MOF MIL-89.55 For this particular MOF, the normal solvothermal synthesis conditions are that iron(III) acetate, transmuconicacid, sodium hydroxide, deionized water, and methanol are first mixed to yield an orange gel. This gel is then aged at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 3 days in a Teflon-lined Parr bomb. This process yields monolithic gels and xerogels consisting of small MIL-89 crystallites embedded in ethanol. As demonstrated by the authors, thin layers of this material can be deposited on solid substrate by a simple dipping procedure. When removing the substrate from the gel, a thin film is left on the surface. After drying a thin $40\ \mathrm{nm}$ film of MOF crystallites remained on the surface. The thickness of the film depends on the viscosity of the gel. The thickness amounted to several $\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . This dip-coating process can be repeated until the deposited MOF film has reached the desired thickness (Fig. 5). MOF thin films prepared using this method are not rigidly grafted to the substrate and are not monolithic, e.g. resemble a sintered powder sample. As a result these MOF membranes exhibit micro- as well as mesoporosity, a property which can have advantages for certain applications (e.g. faster diffusion and permeation through MOF thin films).
## 2.5 Layer-by-layer or liquid phase epitaxy (LPE) of SURMOFs
In contrast to the established synthesis protocols of MOFs, where the reactants (primary building blocks, typically two) are mixed and reacted under solvothermal conditions, also a stepwise layer-by-layer (lbl) or liquid-phase epitaxy (LPE) growth process where the substrate is immersed into solutions of the reaction partners in a sequential, stepwise fashion can be used to synthesize crystalline MOF thin films, as first demonstrated by Shekhah et al.56 This low-temperature deposition of highly porous, oriented metal–organic frameworks exhibiting crystalline order both perpendicular and parallel to the substrate surface is based on the layer-by-layer (lbl) method first employed by Langmuir and Blodgett (LB) for the fabrication of multilayer organic LB films.57 The potential of mixed LB-multilayers has been explored previously by Kuhn and Mo¨ bius,58 and the fabrication of polyelectrolyte-multilayers using a lbl-method has been demonstrated by Decher.59 For a discussion of previous works where the layer-by-layer method has been used to grow other types of (X-ray)-amorphous, porous inorganic materials on substrates see ref. 21. However, in all previous cases of layerby-layer growth of organic/inorganic hybrid materials including coordination polymers, no long range ordering of the deposited multilayers (as demonstrated by well-defined XRD patters showing crystalline order normal and parallel to the substrate) has been reported. Consequently, a rational orientation control of the pore system of multilayer films was not achieved.
In the LPE-process used to fabricate crystalline, porous MOF thin films,56 in the following referred to as ‘‘SURMOFs’’ (i.e.: surface grown, crystalline metal–organic framework multilayers) the individual immersion steps are separated by removing unreacted components via rinsing with a solvent, as shown for the case of HKUST-1 in Fig. 6. The two components, copper(II)acetate $\mathrm{(Cu(ac)}_{2})$ and 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid $(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{btc})$ , were separately dissolved in ethanol and the substrate was immersed into each solution in a cyclic way. Each immersion was followed by rinsing with pure ethanol. By starting with $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{ac})_{2}$ , a linear increase of thickness of the deposited HKUST-1 layer with the number of (alternating) immersion cycles in $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{ac})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{btc}$ is observed when measuring the thickness of the deposited films with appropriate methods, e.g. atomic force microscopy, AFM, infrared spectroscopy or $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray-photoelectron spectroscopy, XPS. A particular advantage of the layer-by-layer method is the possibility to monitor the deposition of both ligands and coupling units not only ex situ using the methods just mentioned but also in situ employing either surface plasmon resonance (SPR)
spectroscopy or a quartz crystal microbalance (QCM) (see Fig. 7). Although the LPE-method is not suitable for all types of MOFs (see e.g. ref. 60) this method has been used successfully for the controlled and selective growth of many different types of MOFs including HKUST-1, $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{ndc})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco})$ , $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco})$ and $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco})$ (dabco $\mathit{\Theta}=\ 1{,}4$ -diazabicyclo[2.2.2]octane), ndc $\mathit{\Pi}=\ 1{,}4$ -naphthalenedicarboxylate) on SAMs with a number of different terminations including COOH, OH and pyridine.61
The thin films grown by the lbl method are highly oriented as demonstrated by $\theta-2\theta$ , or out-of-plane, X-ray diffraction scans. The orientation of the deposited MOF films is found to depend on the functionality of the SAM layer as has been demonstrated for the case of HKUST-1 and $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco}).$ 61 In some cases it has also been possible to control the orientation of the deposited MOF film by changing the sequence of the reaction.62 The MOF thin films fabricated using this LPE process, also referred to as SURMOFs (surface anchored, crystalline and oriented metal–organic framework multilayers), are very homogenous and expose a very flat surface with a roughness on the molecular level; in contrast to the films produced by other methods.20 The LPE method also has the potential to grow types of MOFs not accessible with the solvothermal bulk methods. For example, in the case of MOF-508 non intercalated versions of particular type of MOFs could be obtained, whereas the bulk synthesis scheme always yields one of two different, intercalated pseudomorphs of MOF-508.61,63
It should be noted that the structural quality of the SAMs used as substrates for the SURMOF deposition process plays a crucial role. Low-quality SAMs (see Kind and $\mathsf{W}\ddot{\boldsymbol{\upsigma}}\mathsf{l l}^{47}$ for a discussion of problems with regard to obtaining high-quality organothiolate adlayers on Au-substrates) with a high density of defects, contaminations or structural defects can result in a low quality of the deposited MOF and even in the failure to obtain the growth of crystalline structures.
Recently, the combination of the layer-by-layer method and the Langmuir Blodgett technique has been used for the fabrication of very flat, homogenous and highly ordered and preferentially oriented MOF nano-film supported on a solid surface. The MOF film consists of metallo-porphyrin building units, NAFS-1, and is endowed with a highly crystalline order both in the out-of-plane and in-plane orientations relative to the substrate. The fabricated thin films have a well-controlled growth direction and film thickness.64,65
When applying the step-by-step process to MOFs assembled from more than one ligand, e.g. MOFs of the type $[\mathrm{M}(\mathrm{L})(\mathrm{P})_{0.5}]$ (M: ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ , $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ; L: dicarboxylate linker; P: dinitrogen pillar ligand), LPE growth can be realized by using two types of educt-solutions, one containing the metal source and another one containing a stoichiometric mixture of the ligands) or by using a LPE-process where all reactants are deposited separately. Recently, Zacher et al. demonstrated that the sequence of providing the individual educts does matter and provides an additional means to steer MOF liquid phase epitaxy and in particular to control the orientation of the deposited MOF crystalline film. Just by changing the sequence of the educt deposition it was possible to change the orientation of $\mathrm{[Cu(ndc)(dabco)_{0.5}]}$ SURMOF grown on a pyridine-terminated SAM.62
The highly ordered, crystalline structure as well as the homogeneous, flat morphology of the crystalline, oriented MOF coatings resulting from the layer-by-layer fabrication of SURMOFs create numerous options and thus offer a huge variety of options with regard to creating highly functional, porous surface coatings. Recently, it has been demonstrated by Kreno et al., that the LPE-process can also be applied to grow HKUST-I on Ag nanoparticles66 (see also section 3.6).
## 2.6 Electrochemical synthesis
This method to prepare thin films of HKUST-1 on copper substrates is based on the electrochemical synthesis of MOFs which has been introduced by researchers at BASF.67,68 For the electrochemical synthesis of powdered MOF-5 a copper electrode is immersed in a solution of the organic MOF building block, bdc in the case of MOF-5. By applying an appropriately biased electrical voltage the electrochemical oxidation of $\mathrm{cu}$ -atoms leads to the dissolution of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ metal ions and subsequently to the formation of MOF crystallites in the vicinity of the electrode surface. The continuous supply of more ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ leads to a continuous growth of the crystallites. In the original BASF process the solvent is continuously stirred which leads to a removal of the crystallites formed from the anode surface, thus leading to a continuous production of MOF particles. In a recent paper by Ameloot et al.69 it has been demonstrated that by carefully optimizing the conditions and in the absence of stirring, the electrochemical method can also be used to coat the $\mathrm{cu}$ anode with thin MOF layers. Depending on the voltage and the composition of the solvent thin films of randomly oriented MOF-5 crystallites with sizes of $4{\mathrm{-}}20\upmu\mathrm{m}$ can be grown. This thin films consist of connected crystallites, the thickness of the films correspond to the size of a crystallite. The growth is self-terminating e.g. at a given voltage and composition of the solution the thickness of the film terminates at a particular value. This preparation method also offers the possibility of laterally structuring the deposited MOF films by employing correspondingly structured copper substrates. A disadvantage of the method is that the growth proceeds only on parts of the substrates which are electrically connected.
The latter disadvantage is avoided by another more recently introduced method employing galvanic displacement.70 This method is again based on copper substrates and starts from a mixture of $\mathrm{Ag}(\mathrm{ac})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{btc}$ . Since silver is the more noble metal, ${\mathbf{A}}{\mathbf{g}}^{+}$ ions are reduced at the $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -substrate and exchange with Cu-atoms, yielding a continuous flux of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions into the solution. If the solvent containing ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions and $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{btc}$ acid is evaporated, a rather well defined thin film of partially oriented HKUST-1 crystallites with sizes in the submicrometre region is formed. Although this method is quite elegant is has to be demonstrated in future work how the growth of the films can be controlled in more detail. It has also been shown that by combining this technique with soft lithography it is also possible to form patterned thin MOF-5 films.70
## 2.7 Evaporation induced crystallization
A particularly simple method for producing thin layers of MOF crystallites on a variety of different substrates has recently been proposed by Ameloot et al.71 This method is based on first forming a clear precursor solution (‘‘mother liqueur’’) which does not contain crystallites as discussed in section 2.2 above. These authors used solvents which stabilize the MOF constituents; as a result the formation of small MOF nuclei is avoided. Removing the solvent by slow evaporation leads to crystallization, small MOF crystallites are formed on the bottom of the container.
Interestingly, this method leads to the growth of oriented crystallites, similar to the results reported by Bein and coworkers (see section 2.2). In contrast, however, to the latter results the orientation of the MOF crystallites obtained by evaporation induced crystallization did not depend on the substrate surface termination, for all substrates studied (including silanol, vinyl, or carboxylic acid groups) the deposited crystallites were found to exhibit their (111)-direction normal to the substrate. The roughness of the films created using this method appears to be fairly high, the size of the particles are typically in the range of $1{-}2~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ and thicknesses of $20~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ have been obtained.
## 2.8 Gel-layer synthesis
Very recently, Schoedel, Scherb and $\mathrm{Bein}^{72}$ have used a gel-layer approach to synthesize oriented MOF thin films on modified Au-substrates. Modification of the Au-substrates was done by growing a COOH- or OH-terminated alkanethiolate-based SAM. Subsequently the substrate was coated with a layer of a poly(ethylene oxide) gel, which served as a storage medium for the metal-containing reactants. The formation of the MOF thin films was then induced by pouring solutions of $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{btc}$ (benzenetricarboxylic acid) or $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -bdc (Amino-benzenedicarboxylic acid) on top of the gel layer. A structural analysis of the MOF films using XRD revealed an oriented growth of HKUST-I and the amino-functionalized, flexible framework Fe-MIL-88B- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $\mathrm{[Fe_{3}O(N H_{2}{-}b d c)_{3}C l]}_{n}$ . Interestingly, the gel-layer synthesis of HKUST-1 on differently terminated SAMs, COOH- and OHterminated, yielded the same orientation, with the crystallographic [111] direction orientated perpendicular to the substrate, in contrast to the situation for the direct oriented growth from solvothermal mother solutions, where different orientations were obtained for these two types of SAM terminations (see section 2.2). In the case of $\mathrm{Fe-MIL-88B-NH_{2}}$ the [001]-orientation was found to be normal to the substrate surface; in this case a higher degree of orientation was observed for the COOH-terminated SAM than for the OH-terminated SAM. The thickness of the MOF thin films deposited using this gel-layer method could be varied by adjusting the concentration of the metal-containing reactance within the gel.
## 3. Applications of MOF thin films
In this section we will present examples where MOF thin films have been used to perform specific functions. We will consider successful demonstrations already documented as well as potential applications which have not yet been published. As discussed in the previous paragraph MOF thin films can be prepared using a variety of different methods resulting also in a variety of different properties of the deposited MOF thin films. For example the films can be rather rough consisting of separate crystallites or extremely homogenous with roughness in the order of nm. Not surprisingly for the different applications different types of MOF thin films will be required. In this section a number of different applications will be discussed, either already demonstrated or within sight. In each case we will discuss the requirements on the MOF thin films separately. We will start with optical applications and then come to sensor devices and finally discuss applications in the field of biology.
## 3.1 Luminescence in MOF thin films
3.1.1 Introduction. The ability to load a thin deposited MOF-layer with luminescent material offers a number of interesting options. The most obvious one is the possibility of first loading the MOF layer and then using the UV generated luminescence to check morphology and texture of the deposited MOF film, e.g. to check for holes, incompletely covered areas, cracks and scratches. Moreover, a quantitative analysis of the luminescence signal allows for a rather fast thickness determination. In addition to these more straightforward applications, is the ability to load MOF thin films with luminescent material in a laterally patterned way opens up the possibility to use such materials for optical data storage. Another interesting aspect is a vertical patterning of the MOF thin films, brought about by loading different layers of the MOF with different types of luminescent molecules. Such layered samples, which can be prepared with the LPEmethod, would allow for detailed investigation on fluorescence resonant energy transfer (FRET) between molecules located at different distances from the surface. Also one may envision superlattices of dye molecules embedded in MOFs with interesting applications in the field of optical data storage and photonics. Such applications would require to develop strategies for a selective loading of predefined areas of the deposited MOF thin films, as well as for an anchoring of the dye molecules within the pores so that diffusion processes will not lead to an intermixing.
The presence of lateral and vertical patterns would also allow for the manufacturing of sophisticated sensor devices by employing luminescent molecules which change their optical properties when they get in close proximity to target molecules. Such applications have been demonstrated for another porous material, a zeolite. In a recently published paper Schiffbase-Zn-complexes were grafted to the inner surfaces of pores inside the mesoporous MCM-48 zeolite via formation of covalent bonds. Experimental results revealed that the grafted luminescent molecules could be used for a number of interesting applications by modifying it with a number of different functional groups. In particular it was demonstrated that the fluorescence of the MCM-48 solid infiltrated with Schiff-base groups could be completely quenched by $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ cations. The loaded MCM-48 thus is an interesting realization of a device suited for sensing ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ -cations in aqueous media.73
In this context it should be noted that MOFs not only offer the possibility to be loaded with luminescent molecules but their huge flexibility also allows for a fabrication of MOFs from constituents, coupling units or ligands, which have luminescent properties themselves. With regard to this idea several works have already been reported.74,75 These materials can be prepared by combining the luminescent metal ions or clusters and organic ligands, as well as special guest molecules. For such materials however, few studies for deposition on solid surfaces as thin films has been reported yet. Therefore, in the following we will discuss the fabrication of luminescent MOF thin films and the loading of luminescent molecules into the pores of MOF thin films.
3.1.2 Luminescent MOF thin films. Guo et al.76 have used a lanthanide based MOF to fabricate luminescent MOF thin films for which a number of interesting applications exist. In this study first a suspension of MOF-nanoparticles has been synthesized by applying a capping method to control the size and shape of the MOF particles formed in the conventional solvothermal synthesis process. The nanometre-sized crystals were then used to deposit continuous and smooth ${\mathrm{Eu}}_{1-x}{\mathrm{Tb}}_{x}$ MOF thin films with high mechanical stability employing a spin-coating deposition on ITO-coated glass substrates (Fig. 8).
The fabricated films show a strong luminescence and are characterized by an efficient ${\mathrm{Tb}}^{3+}$ -to- $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ energy transfer (see Fig. 9) and hence open the door for different applications, e.g. with regard to the fabrication of color displays and luminescence sensors.
3.1.3 Luminescence based on inorganic guest molecules in MOF thin films. Shekhah et al. have reported the successful loading of a 3D porous MOF, [HKUST-1],77 with an inorganic dye guest species $\mathrm{[Eu(bzac)_{3}}$ bipy], (bzac $\mathit{\Pi}=\mathit{\Pi}1\mathit{\Pi}$ -phenyl-1,3- butanedionate, bipy $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ bipyridine) (see Fig. 10).78 In this case the loading has been carried out by immersion of the deposited MOF films into ethanolic solutions of the luminescent molecules. XRD data recorded after the loading process reveal the presence of diffraction peaks still located at the same position but with a different intensity ratio. This observation clearly indicates that the luminescent molecules have diffused into the MOF without destroying the porous framework. A simulation of the XRD pattern expected for each pore loaded with one luminescence molecule revealed a very good agreement with the experimental data (Fig. 11). The solid-state absorption spectra of these complexes (Fig. 12) revealed that optical excitation can be transferred from the MOF ligands to the Eu-atoms embedded in the MOF pores.
This successful demonstration opens the possibility to load the deposited MOF thin films not only with one but with several different types of MOF as illustrated in Fig. 13.
In a first step selected layers, e.g. layers 3 and 4 are loaded with one luminescent molecule and then layer 5 and 6 can be loaded with a different dye. Such an arrangement can be used to e.g. study fluorescent resonance and energy transfer (FRET) between dye molecules in a systematic fashion.
## 3.2 QCM-based sensors
One of the most direct applications of MOF thin films is the coating of quartz-substrate as used in quartz crystal-microbalance (QCM) devices. A number of works have already demonstrated that using this approach a reliable method for detecting changes within the deposited thin films and in particular the loading and depletion of these porous coatings with guest molecules can be detected. Due to the rather sophisticated electronic equipment available to measure changes occurring on a quartz crystal microbalance device by detecting small changes in frequency a number of applications can be realized in a straightforward fashion. The most striking advantage of this QCM approach, however, is that the coating of the quartz substrates with MOF thin films is really very simple. A typical QCM-electrode is shown in Fig. 14; it consists of a circular quartz plate with a diameter of $15~\mathrm{mm}$ and thickness of $1\mathrm{mm}$ . The standard electrodes as provided by the manufacturer are coated on both sides with circularly shaped gold electrodes. Such substrates are commonly used in experiments where the amount of deposited material (e.g. in metal deposition) has to be measured or monitored accurately. The approach described above (see section 2.2) for the deposition of MOFs on surfaces, namely using the organic surface exposed by self-assembled monolayers, SAMs,47 as a template for MOF nucleation and growth is directly compatible with the standard QCM electrodes. Simply immersing the (thoroughly cleaned) gold coated quartz electrodes in the ethanolic solution of the corresponding organothiol yields QCM-electrodes where the part metalized with gold is coated with a SAM. The organic surface exposed by the SAM can then be directly used for growing a MOF by using one of the methods described in section 2. The first demonstration that MOF thin films nucleated on a SAM using the crystallization method has been published by Bein et al.79
In this work a 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylate (btc) openframework $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{btc})}_{2}{\cdot}(\mathrm{{H}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}})_{3}$ $x\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (HKUST-1) were deposited on the sensing area of the QCM electrode. The study addressed several key issues related to successful sensor design, including the formation of stable and compact thin films, interfacing with an appropriate transducer concept for sensitive detection, and characterization of the porous films by means of molecular sorption experiments.79
The preparation of a thin film of the HKUST-1 MOF on the gold electrode of a quartz-crystal microbalance (QCM) was achieved by direct growth on a 11-mercaptoundecanol selfassembled monolayer (SAM) (Fig. 15). When such HKUST-1 thin films (film mass between 45 and $71\times10^{-6}\mathrm{g}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ were exposed to water, the quartz crystal microbalance reveals a clear mass uptake revealing the loading of the MOF with water molecules.
As a result the water sorption properties of the deposited MOF material could be studied in a rather straight forward fashion.
Water sorption in MOFs is a topic of general interest in the fields of humidity sensing and of heat transformation for climate control, due to the large pore volume and the high framework stability. A typical water vapor isotherm recorded on the thin film at room temperature $(294~\mathrm{K})$ with the QCM setup is presented in ref. 79. The isotherm-shape was classified as Type IV in the IUPAC classification. The adsorption data were confirmed by the data from the TGA measurement. The amount of water adsorbed at $p/p0\:=\:0.8$ was approximately $16{\mathrm{~mmol~g}}^{-1}$ , which was in accordance with the TGA data. Thus $16\mathrm{\mmol\g^{-1}}$ of water (weight-loss $31\%$ ) are desorbed between room temperature and $130~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Considering that the bulk sample used for the thermo-gravimetric analysis was pretreated under an atmosphere of about $80\%$ relative humidity $(p/p0~=~0.8\$ at $T=294\mathrm{~K~}$ , the two results were consistent.
Note that the fact that the MOF thin films used in this study were polycrystalline and consist of crystallites of many different sizes made it impossible to accurately determine the diffusion constant governing the penetration of the water molecules inside the MOF film from a analysis of the temporal behavior of the mass uptake (see section 2.2).
Ameloot et al. have demonstrated the possibility of using electrochemically synthesized MOF coatings integrated with QCM devices for monitoring water vapor adsorption in a $\mathrm{[Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}]$ thin film grown on top of a quartz crystal microbalance.69 In this study it was demonstrated that changes in relative humidity can be monitored this way and also that the signal is highly reproducible upon cycling between dry and water-containing nitrogen flows. The water sorption capacity of the films was found to be 25–30 wt%.27
In another study a thin film of the HKUST-1 MOF was grown on the gold electrode of a QCM-substrate using the LPE (or layer-by-layer) method by direct growth on a 16-mercaptohexadecanoic acid (MHDA) SAM. The structural quality of the MOF thin films grown on the QCM substrate was verified by XRD and IRRAS measurements which confirm the presence of a highly oriented HKUST-1 SURMOF.80 The loading of the SURMOF with different molecules such as pyridine, heptane and isooctane, was investigated by exposing the coated QCM substrate to the vapor of the corresponding gases using a modified QCM setup.80 Fig. 16 shows the loading of heptane from the gas phase into a HKUST-1 SURMOF grown on a QCMsubstrate. The QCM data shows a very fast increase of the mass with the loading of the heptane in the MOF and reaches equilibrium after $\sim30\ \mathrm{min}$ . The mass uptake measured in the experiment $(250\mathrm{ng}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}.$ ) corresponds to about 9 molecules of heptane per HKUST-1 unit cell. The reversibility of the loading process could be investigated by simply switching back from the heptanes-loaded gas stream to the pure carrier gas stream. The corresponding data showed a fast deloading and indicated a complete desorption of the heptane.81
## 3.3 Measurement of diffusion coefficients in porous metal–organic frameworks
In a recent paper published by Zybaylo et al.,80 it was demonstrated that the use of a QCM substrate covered with a MOF thin film goes far beyond just measuring the amount of adsorbed molecules. In addition to the total amount of frequency change, which converts to the total mass of deposited material, the time dependence can also be measured; which in turn can be used to derive information on the diffusion constants governing the loading of the SURMOF with a particular compound. Note that for this application the rather heterogeneous MOF thin films consisting of crystallites of different sizes – as obtained e.g. with the crystallization method (see section 2.2) – is not suited for this application. A meaningful analysis of the time-dependencies of the adsorption curves is only possible if the MOF thin films exhibit a well-defined thickness.
The first demonstration of this approach to determine diffusion constants in MOFs has been reported by Zybaylo et al.80 The very homogenous, highly ordered MOF thin films grown by liquid phase epitaxy (LPE) or layer-by-layer method (see section 2.5) on SAMs were used. The time-dependence of the mass-uptake of the MOF when exposing it to a gas is measured by a QCM (Fig. 17). The homogenous nature of the MOF thin films together with their well-defined thickness allowed analyzing the QCM-data using Fickian diffusion to yield the diffusion constant. The potential of this method was demonstrated for the case of pyridine diffusion into HKUST-1, for which the diffusion coefficient at room temperature was found to amount to $1.5\ \times\ 10^{-19}\ \mathrm{~m~}^{2}\ \mathrm{~s~}^{1}$ . Assuming a Fickian diffusion and a hopping mechanism, a binding energy of $0.78{\mathrm{~eV}}$ of the pyridine to the $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ sites within the HKUST-1 MOF was obtained, a value in good agreement with the results of precise ab initio quantum chemistry calculations.80
## 3.4 Sensors for stress-induced chemical detection
An elegant application of MOFs to build a novel type of sensor based on the loading-induced changes of MOF lattice parameters has been presented by Allendorf et al.82 If molecules are included in the MOF pores the lattice may shrink or expand as a result of repulsive or attractive interactions of the included molecules with the constituents of the metal–organic framework. This change of geometrical dimensions can be converted to mechanical stress by rigidly depositing the MOF on a solid substrate. The forces resulting from the loading in dual stress can be measured quantitatively by monitoring the deflection of an AFM cantilever. This approach allows creating a highly responsive, reversible, and selective sensor by depositing a thin film of HKUST-1 MOF on a micro cantilever surface. In this first application, the cantilever surface was coated with a HKUST-1 thin film using the two methods described in section 2.2 and 2.5. This sensor responds to water (Fig. 18), methanol, and ethanol vapors, but yields no response to either $\Nu_{2}$ or $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ .
## 3.5 Fabry-perot device as a selective sensor for chemical vapors and gases
If the deposited MOF thin films exhibit a sufficient thickness another cheap and rather straight-forward method can be applied to monitor loading of these porous coatings with guest molecules. As demonstrated by Lu and Hupp,83 the color of a zeolitic imidazolate framework (ZIF) thin film with thicknesses in the micrometre region is determined by interference properties. Depending on the thickness these films exhibit different colors resulting from the constructive and destructive interference of light beams reflected from the upper surface and the substrate (Fig. 19). Since the conditions for constructive and destructive interference depend sensitively not only on the thickness of the deposited film but also on the optical constant of the MOF material itself, loading of the porous thin films by guest molecules can change the color of the thin films even if the other conditions (angle of incidence, etc.) are kept constant. Although this procedure is not as quantitative as the approach using the quartz crystal-microbalance, this method is very attractive since the readout of the sensor is rather straight forward and can be carried out by the naked eye.
Hupp et al. have deposited a zeolitic imidazolic framework, ZIF-8, on a solid substrate using the deposition from mother solution method (see chapter 2.1). Rather homogeneous ZIF-8 thin films were obtained simply by immersing glass or silicon slides into a freshly prepared methanolic mixture of 2-methylimidazole and $Z_{\mathrm{{n}}}({\mathrm{NO}}_{3})_{2}$ at room temperature for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ . Thicker films can be obtained by repeating the process with fresh solutions. The resulting ZIF-8 transparent thin films deposited on either glass or silicon substrate could then be used to optically monitor the Fabry-Perot interference peaks as a function of analyte exposure.83 These interferences are observable when the thickness of a supported material is comparable to the wavelength of the light. Importantly, the wave lengths at which these interferences occur not only depend on the film thickness but also on the films refractive index of the thin film.
Fig. 19 shows a series of ZIF-8 films of various thicknesses, l, grown on silicon substrates. While ZIF-8 itself is colorless, the silicon-supported thin films are not. The variable coloration is a manifestation of film-thickness-dependent optical interference in the visible region. Fabry-Pe´ rot fringes for transmission of visible light through a ca. $1000\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick film (10-cycle growth) of ZIF-8 on glass were monitored to demonstrate the sensing properties. Exposure to propane shifts the visible-region fringes by up to $49\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 20a). The extents of the shifts are sensitive to the propane partial pressure (Fig. 20b). The analyte-induced shift occurs within $1~\mathrm{min}$ and is reversed by replacing the analyte with pure nitrogen gas. From the magnitudes of the shifts in the interference fringes, one can calculate that the volume fraction of analyte in the framework amounts to $\sim0.13$ for pure propane at 1 atm.83
This ZIF-8 optical sensor is sensitive to a range of vapors and gases, and also displaces some chemical selectivity. For example, linear n-hexane is readily sensed, but the sterically more demanding cyclohexane is not. Apparently, the cyclohexane cannot enter the ZIF-8 cavities through the rather small channels characteristic for this type of porous material. The channels of ZIF-8 thus act as filters, molecules exceeding a certain size cannot be loaded in this material, thus creating selectivity.
## 3.6 Fabrication of MOF thin films for localized surface plasmon (LSPR) sensing
Localized surface plasmon resonance (LSPR) spectroscopy is a highly sensitive sensor technique, in most works this technique is applied to for the detections of large biological analytes. Sensing of smaller molecules is a hard target for this technique; applying LSPR spectroscopy to detect hazardous or toxic gases and manage industrial processes involving gaseous chemicals requires a substantial increase in sensitivity of this method. In a recent study by Kreno et al. a novel strategy based on MOF-coatings has been reported. Briefly, the Ag-nanoparticles constituting the plasmonic substrate were coated with a HKUST-1 MOF thin film, using the layer-by-layer or LPE-method.66
Fig. 21b shows an SEM image of $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ nanoparticles array coated with 20 cycles of HKUST-1. The MOF film is highly crystalline and forms a dense coating covering both, the metal particles and the glass substrate. Larger MOF crystallites follow the pattern of the underlying nanoparticle array, implying that the –COOH terminated SAM deposited on the Ag nanoparticles templates the growth of large HKUST-1 crystals. Note, that smaller crystallites also grow on the bare glass surface. While the alkane-thiolate based SAM is not expected to form on the glass substrate, the coverslip substrates used in these experiments have initially been treated with a basic solution to promote electrostatic self-assembly of the polystyrene nanospheres. Thus, $-\mathrm{OH}$ groups are likely to be present on the glass surface, thus providing nucleation sites for MOF crystallization, as has been reported on $-\mathrm{OH}$ terminated SAMs.84 The analysis of the X-ray diffraction patterns recorded for the MOF-coated plasmonic substrate revealed that HKUST-1 was indeed coated on the surfaces of the $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ nanoparticles.66
Gas sensing experiments were first performed with nonfunctionalized Ag nanoparticles arrays (Fig. 21a). Prior to each experiment, the sample was purged overnight with dry ${\bf N}_{2}$ . Subsequently, a test gas ( $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{SF}_{6})$ was dosed in $60\mathrm{~s~}$ pulses, separated by $60\mathrm{~s~}$ pulses of ${\bf N}_{2}$ . When the test gas was switched on, ${\bf N}_{2}$ was simultaneously switched off, such that only one gas flowed over the sample at a time. Fig. 22a shows the sensor response to both test gases for five repeated pulses. When the test gas was introduced (at $t=30~\mathrm{s}$ ), a red shift was observed as an increase in $\Delta\lambda_{\mathrm{max}}$ . This corresponds to the higher refractive indices of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and ${\mathrm{SF}}_{6}$ (1.000449 RIU and 1.000703 RIU, respectively) relative to ${\bf N}_{2}$ (1.000298 RIU). Upon purging the sample cell with ${\bf N}_{2}$ , the LSPR peak blue-shifted back to its baseline value.
To probe the effect of the MOF film, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sensing procedure was repeated for $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ particles functionalized with HKUST-1. Fig. 22b shows results for the MOF/NP sample with the maximum response to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ overlaid with the same experiment using bare nanoparticles. In the bare particle case, $\Delta\lambda_{\mathrm{max}}$ amounts to $0.13\mathrm{nm}$ . The sensor coated with 37 cycles of KHUST-1 exhibits a $\Delta\lambda_{\mathrm{max}}$ of $1.88\mathrm{nm}$ , a 14-fold larger signal. This significant signal enhancement can be rationalized considering that HKUST-1 readily sorbs larger amounts of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ than $\Nu_{2}$ . At the operating pressure ( $\sim1$ atm), the MOF may adsorb almost 10 times more $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ than ${\bf N}_{2}$ .
To further corroborate the hypothesis that the MOF is selectively concentrating $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , Kreno et al. studied the dependence of the signal enhancement on MOF film thickness.66 As shown in Fig. 23a, $\Delta\lambda_{\mathrm{max}}$ increases with increasing number of MOF growth cycles. As the free space around the nanoparticles is filled with MOF, more $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is expected to be concentrated within the sensing volume, producing an increase in $\Delta\lambda_{\mathrm{max}}$ Since the plasmon-induced electromagnetic field decays exponentially from the nanoparticles surface, molecules closest to the nanoparticles have the greatest effect on $\Delta\lambda_{\mathrm{max}}$ . Thus, $\Delta\lambda_{\mathrm{max}}$ is expected to increase rapidly for low numbers of growth cycles, when new MOF growth is closest to the surface. The increase of sensitivity should then level off for larger MOF thicknesses. Fig. 23b demonstrates that the experimental observation is in full accord with these predictions.
## 3.7 Fabrication of MOF thin films membranes
Since the permeation of gas through a powder material is difficult to measure for a number of applications in the area of gas separation it would be highly desirable to have available mm-sized, monolithic MOF-membranes. Such a fabrication of continuous MOF-5 membranes supported on a porous substrate was achieved by Yoo et al.54 by employing a secondary growth method. Densely packed MOF-5 seed layers deposited on porous $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -alumina substrates were rapidly prepared using microwave induced thermal deposition (MITD) method (see section 2.3). In a second step the MOF-5 seed layers were immersed in the regular MOF-5 synthesis solution where the nuclei formed continued to grow eventually yielding continuous, monolithic MOF-5 membranes, see Fig. 24. The presence of a proton scavenging amine in precursor solutions during the secondary growth step was found to be critical to prevent the seed crystals from dissolving, thus enabling the growth of the MOF seed crystals into a continuous film. For the first time, preferentially oriented MOF-5 membranes were prepared by growing from oriented MOF-5 seed layers. As a result of the porous structure of the substrate the MOF thin film permeation experiments could be carried out for these MOF-5 membranes in a straight forward fashion (see Fig. 26). Analysis of this data revealed in Knudsentype transport of the small gas molecules through the membrane.
The permeation (see Fig. 25) of small gas molecules through randomly oriented continuous MOF-5 membranes fabricated using this method was measured using a time-lag method.
Before the measurement, as-prepared MOF-5 membranes were activated using a defined procedure. In short, after thoroughly washing MOF-5 membranes with a fresh DMF solution three times, the membranes were placed in a Soxhlet extractor in which $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ was refluxed for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ to exchange DMF. The solvent exchanged MOF-5 membranes were kept at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ in a vacuum oven to remove $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ .5 $\mathbf{\bar{\rho}}_{\mathbf{N}_{2}}.$ -adsorption and TGA for activated MOF-5 powder samples confirmed that after the activation process the pores of the MOF-5 samples are available for the inclusion of small molecules.
Fig. 26 (left) shows the permeance of gas molecules through a membrane as a function of their molecular weight. First, the permeation through graphite-coated $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -alumina substrates was compared with that of bare $\mathfrak{a}$ -alumina, confirming that there is no resistance to permeation of gas molecules due to the presence of thin graphite powders on the substrate surface. Both substrates show Knudsen diffusion behavior i.e., the permeance is proportional to 1/Mwt. Permeation of gas molecules through the activated MOF-5 membranes indicates the behavior of the Knudsen diffusion process, which is consistent with Lai and coworkers’ result (Fig. 26 (right)). It is speculated that the Knudsen type transport of small gas molecules through MOF-5 membranes is due to relatively large pores ( $\mathrm{\sim}1.4~\mathrm{nm})$ of MOF-5 (see Fig. 27) as compared to the size of gas molecules (less than $0.4~\mathrm{nm}$ ).
Also Caro and coworkers have reported the fabrication of several types of continuous ZIFs membranes supported on different substrates.42,85–87 The membranes fabricated using a combination of the methods described in section 2.1 and 2.3 were grown on porous alumina disks (Inocermic) (see Fig. 28) and successfully used as molecular sieves, e.g. for hydrogen separation.42,88
Directly after the synthesis process the ZIF-7 membranes were found to be impermeable to all gases, including $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , since the pores and the channels in the framework are still blocked by solvent molecules. An on-stream activation was carried out using a Wicke-Kallenbach permeation cell with a $1{:}1$ mixture of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and ${\bf N}_{2}$ on the feed side. Fig. 29 represents the variation of the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and the ${\bf N}_{2}$ permeance from their binary mixture during the on-stream activation process.
As confirmed by the thermogravimetric (TG) analysis, the guest molecules began to leave the cavities within the MOF membranes when the cell was heated up to $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , at which point the ZIF-7 membrane became permeable to the feed gas. At $165^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ the partially activated ZIF-7 membranes showed a $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ permeance of about $7\times10^{-9}\mathrm{\mol\m^{-2}\ s^{-1}P a^{-1}}$ and a $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ separation factor of 8. A complete activation was achieved after maintaining the temperature at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for around $40\mathrm{~h~}$ Then the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ permeance reached a plateau value of approximately to $8\ \times$ $\mathrm{\bar{10}^{-8}}\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{m}^{-2}\mathrm{s}^{-1}\mathrm{Pa}^{-1}\mathrm{\bar{\it$ nd the $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ separation factor was 7.7.
For applications at elevated temperature, the activated ZIF-7 membrane was tested in single-gas and mixed-gas permeation at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ using the Wicke-Kallenbach technique. Fig. 30 gives the permeance of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , ${\bf N}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ as single gases as well as from their $1{:}1$ mixtures through the ZIF-7 membrane as a function of the kinetic diameter of the gas molecules. For both single- and mixed-gas permeation, there is a clear cut-off between $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . The $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ ideal selectivity (calculated as the ratio of single-gas permeance) and separation factor are 6.7 and 6.5, respectively, which exceed the Knudsen separation factor (ca. 4.7).
This study has shown that MOF membranes made from ZIF-7 exhibit several advantages. First, the pore dimension approaches the size of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , allowing to obtain high $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ selectivities without any sophisticated pore-size engineering as is essential for zeolite membranes targeting $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ separation. Second, ZIF-7 membranes are thermally very stable and make gas separations at high temperatures possible (ZIF-7 is stable at least to $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . Finally, the hydrophobic nature of this porous material endows it with a very good hydrothermal stability.88
Qui et al. also successfully fabricated $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}$ membranes supported on copper net by means of a twin copper source.89 In comparison with the conventional zeolite membranes, the copper net supported $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}$ membrane has exhibited higher permeation flux and selectivity for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ .
## 3.8 Preparing optical thin films of nanoporous metal–organic frameworks from colloids
A simple route for the preparation of MOF thin films with high optical quality starting from a colloidal solution which is then coated onto a substrate has been presented by Ferey and coworkers.55 The authors demonstrated the potential of their method for the flexible porous iron carboxylate MOF MIL-89. A modified synthesis process yields monolithic gels and xerogels which are suited for a simple dip-coating process. The properties of the deposited thin films were investigated using environmental ellipsometry. The high versatility of the particular MOFs used, MIL-88 and MIL-89, results in very interesting properties of the deposited thin films. In particular the possibility to tailor their sorption probabilities opens the path for many applications.
MIL-89 is a highly flexible MOF that can—accompanied by a reversible increase of the pore volume—adsorb polar liquids. The swelling of the films was studied by environmental ellipsometry. This method allows monitoring the refractive index and the thickness of the deposited MOF film in situ, in addition to static properties, dynamic variations can also be measured. An interesting example is the inclusion of water molecules, which can be studied simply by varying the relative humidity (RH) of the ambient. In one set of experiments the relative vapor pressure of water was modulated between $0\%$ and $100\%$ inside the analysis chamber while the variation in thickness associated with water adsorption was recorded simultaneously, constituting a direct measurement of the volume-swelling ability. Fig. 31 shows the variation in thickness, volume, and corresponding refractive index of a single- and a double-layer MIL-89 coating with an initial thickness of $40~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ and $80~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ , respectively, upon submission to varying RHs. One can clearly see that both systems exhibit the expected reversible swelling property, with a relative swelling at high humidity of $200\%$ and $150\%$ for the single and the double layer, respectively. The swelling is accompanied by a decrease in the refractive index from 1.65 (contracted, empty organometallic framework) to 1.45 (mixture of inorganic framework at 1.65 and water at 1.33, $\lambda~=~700~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ ). Because of the hydrophobic nature of the linkers the swellings was observed only at rather high humidities (RH larger $90\%$ ). Remarkably, the layers contracted back to their initial thickness when reducing the relative humidity, suggesting that the loading with water is reversible and does not lead to a modification of the topology of the MOF lattice or the deposited MOF thin film.
## 3.9 Hybrid films of isoreticular metal–organic frameworks
A particularly interesting option in connection with the deposition of MOF thin films on surfaces is to grow a second type of MOF on top of such a porous thin coating. The motivation to fabricate such hybrid films can range from adjusting the surface properties of the MOF coating (e.g. to generate a hydrophobic capping on top of a more hydrophilic $\mathbf{MOF}^{62})$ to adding selectivity to MOF membranes deposited on porous substrates. For bulk MOFs, the possibility to obtain such MOF-on-MOF growth has already been demonstrated e.g. by Kitagawa and coworkers.90–92 Yoo and Yeong have reported the heteroepitaxial growth of isoreticular metal–organic frameworks (IRMOFs), i.e. the successful growth of one type of IRMOF on small crystallites of another type of IRMOF.93 The size of the seed crystals onto which the second type of MOF was deposited amount to about $150~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . The presence of an amine compound in the growth solution was found to be critical for the heteroepitaxial growth of the IRMOFs, in the absence of the amine compound the IRMOF seed crystals started to dissolve when beginning with the epitaxial deposition of the second type of IRMOF. Single-crystal $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{X}$ -ray diffraction data proved the epitaxy of the shell crystals grown on the surface of the core crystals (Fig. 32). IRMOF hybrid films were also prepared on porous $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -alumina supports by growing IRMOF-3 crystals on the surface of IRMOF-1 seed crystal layers which in a first step had been deposited on the porous $\mathfrak{a}$ -Alumina substrates by method described in section 2.1. These results are expected to enable the preparation of a series of novel hybrid IRMOFs and their thin films and membranes with multi-functionalities and improved properties for advanced applications.93
In another study Zacher et al.62 have shown that the MOF-on-MOF method is also compatible with the liquid phase epitaxial deposition process of depositing SURMOFs on organic surfaces. In this work three component MOFs, i.e. MOFs consisting of one metal ion node and two different type of ligands with the general structure $[\mathrm{M}(\mathrm{L})(\mathrm{P})_{0.5}]$ (M: ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ , $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ; L: dicarboxylate linker; P: di-nitrogen pillar ligand), were deposited on different types of SAMs. It was demonstrated that by optimizing reaction conditions an heteroepitaxial growth could be achieved in a straightforward fashion, e.g. the growth of 20 layers of $[(\mathrm{Cu})(\mathrm{bdc})(\mathrm{dabco})_{0.5}]$ on top of a 20 layer $[(\mathrm{Cu})(\mathrm{bdc})(\mathrm{dabco})_{0.5}]$ SURMOF.62
## 3.10 Preparation of free-standing MOF platelets
A useful and a practical method to efficiently delaminate SURMOF platelets with predefined shapes and well-defined thicknesses grown by selective liquid-phase epitaxy on prestructured organic substrates was developed by Wo¨ ll and coworkers.94 The method is based on a lift-off process utilizing PMMA (polymethyl methacrylat) as an adhesive to delaminate SURMOFs without affecting the high crystalline order of these porous materials. In this study, HKUST-1 was first grown selectively using the layer-by-layer method on an organic surface laterally structured using micro-contact printing $(\upmu\mathrm{CP})$ . This process yielded HKUST-1 squares grown on the COOH-terminated squares (made from 16-mercaptohexadecanoic acid, MHDA). No deposition was found to occur on the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ -terminated stripes (made from hexadecane thiol (HDT)).56
After the deposition the substrate was covered with small drops of the polymer PMMA dissolved in chlorobenzene. Subsequent heating of the PMMA-coated samples on a hot plate for $5\mathrm{min}$ at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ resulted in the formation of a solid film of PMMA resin. This rather stable polymer film could then be removed from the substrate using a razor blade. Dissolution of the PMMA in acetone then yielded suspended SURMOF squares. The delamination procedure is shown schematically in Fig. 33. After the dissolution of the PMMA resin small drops of the acetone solution containing the suspended delaminated MOF squares were positioned from a syringe on the Formvar-covered side of a TEM grid. After drying in air for about $1~\mathrm{min}$ the delaminated squares were investigated by TEM and energy-dispersive X-ray (EDX) analysis. In addition, a scanning electron microscope (SEM)
and an optical microscope have been used to investigate the delamination from the substrate and the delaminated MOF on the TEM grid (see Fig. 34). Clearly, the mechanical removal of the PMMA resin has resulted in a complete delamination of the MOF squares. Fig. 34b depicts a TEM micrograph of delaminated HKUST-1 squares. The sample shows no evidence of fracture, deformation, coalescence, extensive cracking, or structural collapse, indicating that the structure of the framework remains intact during the delamination process.94
At present, studies are under way to investigate whether the uniformly shaped MOF platelets can be used as seed particles in connection with the conventional hydrothermal synthesis of MOFs. Another interesting application is to use them as nano-containers like zeolites,95 for entrapping metal nanoparticles or for the loading with molecular dyes. Another interesting application would be to use these freestanding, monolithic membranes for gas permeation experiments.
## 3.11 Applications as model systems
As for any other porous materials, detailed insights at the microscopic level into the complexity of the MOFs are essential in order to obtain more detailed insight into the complexity of the interaction between host molecules and the inner surfaces of the pores constituting the MOFs, such information is essential with regard to obtaining a thorough understanding of the different types of interactions which are possible between guest molecules and the host lattice. Compared to zeolites, the necessity to obtain such information is even more crucial for MOFs because of the fact that the lattice of the MOFs is considerably more flexible than the zeolites and—for many host molecules—the host lattice has been shown to expand or contract as a reaction of the inclusion of guest molecules.16 In practice, however, a number of complications arise when performing experiments to unravel the interactions of guest molecules with the powder material typically obtained by the MOF synthesis process. In a normal experiment it is not straightforward to distinguish between molecules adsorbed in the inner pores of the porous material or at the outer surface of MOF particles. In addition, some of the standard analytical techniques cannot be applied to powder material in a straightforward fashion. Whereas application of infrared spectroscopy in transmission through MOF powder particles does not cause any problems, the application of photoelectron-spectroscopy to MOF thin films is severely hampered by charging problems and by the difficulty caused from background signals. In this context highly homogenous MOF thin films deposited on a conducting substrate—possibly in an oriented fashion—offer a number of advantages since virtually all techniques available in surface science can be applied to study such thin films.
An instructive example illustrating the potential of MOF for such systematic studies are detailed investigations on the incorporation of carbon monoxide, CO, into MOFs of type HKUST-1. Within the empty framework the CO molecules can bind to the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ within the bulk structure (see Fig. 35). Since the CO stretch vibration $\nu_{1}$ depends rather sensitively on the chemical nature of the metal ion to which the molecule is bound the $\nu_{1}$ frequency can be used as a sensitive probe to investigate the metal different sites exposed at the inner surfaces of the pores accessible within the porous MOF structure. In the ideal structure, see Fig. 35, only $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ sites are exposed. In the real structure, in addition to the perfect ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sites in the paddlewheel $\mathrm{Cu}$ -dimers, of course also other species will be present, e.g. Cu-ions in the vicinity of defects such as vacancies or imperfections. In addition, Cu-ions will be exposed at the outer surfaces of the MOF crystallites or MOF thin films which are expected to have different properties from those in the bulk. Finally, also Cu-species in different oxidation state have to be expected, e.g. $\mathrm{Cu}^{+}$ in $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ .
Previous studies reported by Zecchina and coworkers96 for bulk powder sample of HKUST-1 have already demonstrated that instead of the expected single vibrational band of CO bound to only one type of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ -species a number of different bands are seen after exposing the MOF material to CO at low temperatures. These additional bands have to be attributed to the presence of different types of $\mathrm{cu}$ ions present in the MOF bulk, indicating the presence of a fairly large amount of defects. Since the powder particles used in this study of Zecchina and coworkers96 were fairly small the outer surface exposed by these particles is considerable and will very likely contribute numerous defect sites. Since, in IR spectroscopy, it is not possible to distinguish between surface and bulk sites an unambiguous assignment of the additional CO vibrations bands is not straightforward. In this context high quality MOF thin films grown on a solid, highly reflective and electrically conductive substrate offer a number of advantages over powder samples. First, they allow applying grazing incidence infrared spectroscopy (RAIRS, reflection absorption infrared spectroscopy) for the investigation of the films in a quantitative fashion. Second, in the thin, highly oriented quasi-epitaxial layers the number of defects and grain boundaries is substantially reduced with regard to the bulk powder samples. Third, the number of binding sites for CO available at the outer surface of the MOF film is constant and—to first approximation—does not depend on MOF film thickness. Therefore investigations carried out for SURMOFs offer the potential to distinguish between these different adsorption sites in a straightforward fashion. Below we will demonstrate these advantages using a case study for HKUST-I thin films exposed to CO molecules.
In Fig. 36 we show series of IR spectra recorded for CO embedded within a HKUST-1 thin film prepared by the layerby-layer or LPE method. IR spectra recorded for a 40-layer HKUST-1 SURMOF grown on a MHDA-SAM are shown in Fig. 36(a) and (b). Upon CO dosing, two bands at $2179\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $2124~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are observed. Following the assignment in previous studies96 on the adsorption of CO in HKUST-1 powder materials, the intense band at $2179~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is ascribed to the reversible formation of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{CO}}$ adducts inside the HKUST-1 sample. The second band at $2124~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ is assigned to $\mathrm{CO}$ bound to $\mathrm{Cu}^{+}$ ions. Zecchina and coworkers have proposed that the presence of $\mathrm{Cu}^{+}$ ions results from the formation of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ as a result of heating.
In fact, for the CO adsorption studies in bulk HKUST-1 sample, before recording the IR-data, the powder samples were activated by heating to $453\mathrm{K}$ in order to remove residual water molecules present in the pores of the material. According to Zecchina and coworkers, during this heating a fraction of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions are reduced to $\mathrm{{Cu}^{+}}$ .96 On the basis of the present data we feel that we can rule out this hypothesis because the HKUST-1 SURMOFs grown on an MHDM-SAM at room temperature have not been heated to higher temperatures. While we agree with Zecchina et al. in that the band at $2127~\mathrm{{cm}}~^{-1}$ has to be assigned to CO-species bound to $\mathrm{Cu}^{+}$ ions the source of these impurities cannot be the heating process but must result from $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ impurities either contained in the Cu-acetate solution used for the synthesis or formed by oxidation when exposing the MOF thin films to the ambient.
In order to study this behavior in more detail the HKUST-1 thin films were heated to different temperatures and then characterized by $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray photoelectron-spectroscopy (XPS). Note, that because of the small film thickness ( $(60\ \mathrm{nm}$ for 40-layers HKUST-1 SURMOF) charging effects which cause severe problems when recoding XPS data for powder samples are strictly absent and data can be recorded in a straightforward fashion. The corresponding results are presented in Fig. 37, and Table 1 lists the assignments and fitting results of the Cu $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ XPS peak.
In the pristine HKUST-1/MHDA/Au sample the sample contains $12.9\%$ $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . Upon heating to $373\mathrm{K}$ and $420~\mathrm{K}$ , the fractions of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in the sample increase to $16.2\%$ and $41.9\%$ , respectively. The XPS data are thus consistent with the previous studies that upon heating, the fraction of the Cu2+ is reduced to $\mathrm{Cu}^{+}$ but clearly demonstrates that a large fraction of $\mathrm{Cu}^{+}$ species is already present in the pristine HKUST-I material.
The CO adsorption in bulk HKUST-1 is rather complex, a number of new bands is observed when exposing the MOF material to CO, in addition to the bands at $2179~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $2124~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ . All IR spectra presented in Fig. 33 were recorded by using a deuterated hexadecane-thiol SAM on $\mathrm{{Au/Si}}$ as a reference, the normal procedure for normalizing IRRAS-data for organic thin films on Au substrates. Since some of the vibrational frequencies of $\mathrm{CD}_{2}$ lie in the regions of CO stretching and might lead to complications in the interpretation of new features induced by CO adsorption within the MOF, for the spectra shown in Fig. 38a different reference sample was used for the normalization of the data, namely empty HKUST-1 thin films grown on MHDA/Au. The corresponding results are shown in Fig. 38, no additional bands are observed but the bands at $2179~\mathrm{{cm}}^{-1}$ and $2124~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , thus the HKUST-1/MHDA/Au sample are more appropriate to study the adsorption behaviors of such small molecules than bulk HKUST-1.
In future experiments we plan to use other standard methods in surface science to characterize MOF thin films, in particularly thermal desorption spectroscopy (TDS) in order to accurately determine the binding energy of small molecules like $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ inside the pores of the MOF.97 Also in this case we expect a significant advance in the quality of the data by using well defined thin MOF films (Fig. 39), which are in excellent thermal contact with a metal substrate.
## 3.12 Mechanistic studies on nucleation and growth of MOFs
Recently Shekhah et al. used the in situ SPR monitoring of the lbl growth of MOFs on SAMs in order to perform mechanistic studies of the nucleation and growth of MOFs.84 A particular question addressed in these studies was that on the importance of so-called secondary building units, or SBUs,16 with regard to the MOF growth process. A characteristic feature of the LPE process described in section 2.5 is the fact that the reactants are offered separately. As a result, the role of SBUs can be compared directly with the results of other studies on growth phenomena on MOF surfaces.98,99 For a more detailed discussion of such growth phenomena see the review by Zacher et al.21
The results of a case study on HKUST-1 deposition on a 16-mercaptohexdecanoic acid SAM are displayed in Fig. 40. The SPR data reveal that virtually no deposition of MOF is observed on a MOF substrate when using copper nitrate as a precursor. At first sight this is surprising, because a similar exchange of the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ion sources does not affect the MOF synthesis under solvothermal conditions ( $105^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ 1 atm). In fact the standard solvothermal process to produce HKUST-1 is based on $\mathrm{Cu}$ -nitrate,100 only recently the successful synthesis of this material starting from Cu-acetate has been reported.101 If, after several cycles involving exposure to copper nitrate, the substrate is exposed to copper acetate again, a very regular deposition is observed (Fig. 40), with growth rates undistinguishable from those seen before switching to the copper nitrate. This finding can be rationalized by considering that in solutions of copper acetate the dominant unit present is the paddle wheel, a fourfold acetate-bridged ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ dimer $\mathrm{\small{[Cu_{2}(C H_{3}C O O)_{4}(H_{2}O)_{2}]}}$ (see inset in Fig. 40).84 This is important, since the $\mathrm{[Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}]$ framework is composed of very similar, btc-bridged ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ dimers, the only modification necessary for the growth process is an exchange of the acetate groups by the carboxylic acid groups of the btc ligands. In case of Cu-nitrate, the solution does not contain $\mathrm{Cu}$ -dimers but the structural chemistry of solvated copper(II) nitrate compounds is rather diverse, but nitrate-bridged dinuclear paddle-wheel species have never been reported, neither in the solid state nor in solution.84
In order to further investigate the general importance of preformed SBUs for the growth of $\mathrm{[Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}]$ crystallites in this study also the dependence of growth rate along different crystallographic directions of HKUST-1 could be investigated. The possibility to do such experiments arises from the fact that HKUST-1 MOF thin films grow in different orientations on different organic substrates. The same study was done on 16- mercaptoundecanol84 and the SPR data shown in Fig. 40 reveals that the deposition rate on the OH-terminated organic surface is somewhat delayed and also the maximum growth rate under steady-state conditions is about half that on the [100] oriented surface. Despite the substantial variation of the growth rates for the two different crystallographic orientations, again no significant growth is seen for copper nitrate on the [111] surface.
## 4. Conclusions
Creating highly functional interfaces using flexible and hierarchical architectures combining surface-functionalization strategies with elements from supramolecular chemistries clearly carries a lot of potential with regard to Interfacial Systems Chemistry (IFSC).1 Thin films of metal–organic frameworks, MOFs, deposited on suitable substrates clearly carry a lot of potential with regard to a realization of highly complex, multifunctional interfaces with, ultimately, switchable properties. The functionality of MOFs results from their extreme flexibility—myriads of combinations of the metal- or metal-oxo coupling units with appropriately functionalized organic linkers create a huge basis of MOF-structures equipped with already an enormous variety of chemical, catalytic and electronic properties. The ability to load the MOFs after their synthesis with functional molecules (dyes, molecular magnets, metal clusters) further extends the number of options offered by these molecular frameworks.
The prospects offered by this class of highly porous, stable and functional materials is already fascinating and has been the subject of numerous review articles.12,15,16,74 The works reviewed in this article demonstrate that the deposition of thin MOF films on solid substrates significantly extends the already quite large number of MOF applications and is well suited for creating the hierarchical interfacial structures required in IFSC. Since the field is moving very fast, we can provide here only a momentary view, presently each month new papers describing novel applications appearing. In the review we have focused on the preparation of MOF thin films and their applications, the very interesting prospects of MOF surface chemistry have been discussed in another recent review article.21
Studying the different examples presented on the preceding pages reveals that a number of opportunities, both in research and applications, are created by depositing MOF thin films on surfaces. This is particularly true for SURMOFs, surface attached, crystalline and oriented MOF multilayers grown by an LPE process which exhibit the same structural and chemical properties including permanent porosity and reversible adsorption capability as known for the bulk compounds. The main advantages of MOF thin films are:
(1) MOF thin films deposited on a conducting substrate are   
well suited for an application of standard surface science   
methods. (2) MOF thin films deposited on a quartz crystal micro  
balance substrate immediately allow studying adsorption   
within these porous materials using simple setups; in addition   
the measurement of diffusion coefficients becomes possible. (3) MOF thin films deposited on Au-substrates allow for a   
straightforward application of SPR-spectroscopy. (4) The presence of a homogenous, flat substrate with   
defined optical properties opens up a number of optical   
applications. (5) Employing the substrate as an electrode opens up the   
way for electrical and electrochemical applications. (6) Using layer-by-layer methods, and in particular the   
LPE-process, for fabricating SURMOFs extends the already   
huge flexibility of MOFs even further by allowing to produce
superlattices consisting of different types of MOF-layers stacked in an arbitrary sequence.
(7) Free-standing, monolithic MOF thin films produced by a delamination process creates yet different prospects with regard to using them as membranes for e.g. gas-separation
In addition, the review also demonstrated the possibility of using MOF thin films as model systems for detailed studies on MOF-related phenomena which are of principal interest for bulk (or powder) MOF materials, e.g. adsorption and diffusion of small molecules into MOFs as well as the formation mechanism of MOFs.
